  "$comment_nontext": "SC 1.4.11 expectations for the non-text pass in e2e/contrast-painted.spec.ts, added by ADR 007. A finding here is a focus indicator or an accent-painted graphical object under 3:1 that is recorded with a reason rather than fixed. Same rule as the painted-contrast record above: an entry that stops reporting is stale and fails the gate.",
  "nonTextContrastExpectations": {
    "recordedOn": "2026-09-07",
    "findings": [
      "Toast/with-content dark: button (focus)",
      "Toast/with-content light: button (focus)"
    ],
    "reason": "The fixture's own `<button>Undo</button>`, passed through Toast's `content` prop — the library styles the toast, not the arbitrary nodes a consumer puts inside it, so this is Chromium's default focus ring at 1.75:1 on the toast surface. Recorded rather than fixed because styling it would be the fixture pretending to be the library. It is a real thing a consumer can do: a bare button inside a toast gets the UA ring.",
    "unindicated": [],
    "unindicatedReason": "Empty, and that is the finding: every focusable element in every fixture shows a focus indicator in both themes. The 757 this pass first reported were rings it could not read — a transparent placeholder layer taken for the ring colour, a baseline snapshotted while the element was already focused, an indicator drawn by focus-within on an ancestor, and oklab() colours, which Tailwind v4 emits for its own palette. Five library elements did genuinely have none and were given the standard ring; see ADR 007."
  }
}
